Subject: organizations

>This whole discussion parallels the theory of the firm in economics. 

>It started with a famous article by Ronald Coase (The Nature of the 

>Firm) that asked, in essence, if markets are so great why have firms 

>at all?

Why encapsulate anything?  Why create functions and objects?  I think  
that the answer involves a concept of packaging, of creating  
effective interfaces.  If it is important for a programmer to be able  
to use an object or function without having to know its internal  
organization, then why is it not important for a client to use a  
firm's product or service without having to know its internal  
organization.  


Reducing the descriptive complexity of a program (and programming  
library) makes programmers more efficient; reducing the descriptive  
complexity of a commercial organization (and the underlying network  
of organizations, i.e., the economy) makes consumers/producers more  
efficient.

-Pete
