From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Mike Price suggests:
>Both these requirements are met by flooding a volume with holes, or more
>precisely with high capacity holes.  The capacity of a hole is the
>throughput a hole can sustain before it collapses.  If rogue holes
>invade an area to redefine local empire time it becomes a battle of
>might.  A perimeter of really big holes would secure a volume against
>invasion.  Any smaller holes, connected to a different empire time,
>would burn out as they approached.  Provided the defender holes were big
>enough they could absorb the energy flux that such encounters create as
>a temporary hiccup.

Two wormhole ends, one small and one big, each going very close to the
speed of light but in opposing directions, start to form a time
machine, even though they are very distant in space/time.  Do they a) both
collapse b) the small hole collapses, while the big hole flys on after
suffering a burst of stuff through it, c) repel and therefore bounce
off each other?  Unless these holes are *very* rigid, such a high speed
"bounce" sounds more like a "smash" to me, destroying both.  Only the
b scenario gives your idea.

>The front of an expanding sphere
>of civilisation would be holes travelling at close to light speed,
>leaving a trail of smaller holes behind, like buoys on a fishing net
>tossed over the side of moving trawler, that grow in size as colonists
>move in, bringing more holes with them.  The only holes destroyed as two
>civilisation meet will be the in-flight holes.  The holes trailed behind
>would be left in operation.  This would define a natural merging of
>empire time up to the Neutral Zone.
>
>> [...] And with massive hole destruction, arranging meetings for
>> negotiations might take a while in empire time.  Here's a
>> good Schelling point problem - where should hole ends meet to merge
>> the nets?
>
>Where/when better than the neutral zone itself.  

Say the empires meet at one billion light years from their centers.
At that distance, how far apart will the bouys dropped off be?  You'd
need a damn lot of bouys to even space them one million light years
apart.  So all the sudden your farthest hole collapses, and so your
next farthest bouy is currently decelerating to a position about a
million light years from your estimated point of last contact.  Same
for the other guys.  But in order to merge your hole networks, the two
bouys must meet in *exactly* the same spot, not just within thousands
of light years of each other.  They must avoid "passing" each other
and thereforing destroying at least one of them.  And the longer they
bouys spend at slower speeds looking around or searching, the more
empire time is being used up.  25 empire years to first detection, and
another 100 empire years to first actual meeting doesn't sound at all
implausible to me.  Seems to me the best bet would be to just pick
prominent points, like quazars, and send bouys to slow down to stop
there, hoping they picked the same spot.  Hence a classic Schelling
point problem.

>Connecting up with other
>civilisations would be the only way of breaking out of the future light
>cone defined by local empire time.  By cooperating with other
>civilisations we meet with we can travel to regions of space time that
>would otherwise be forever barred by relativity.  Trying to invade them
>with *our* wormholes would reduce the space-time available to us.

Hmm.. what they might want best is to invade *through* the wormholes
and take over.  Another reason not to go tossing bouys around like
confetti.

>> For peacetime trading, a tremendous
>> "time wind" (wind of change?) would allow much faster velocities
>> (relative to empire time) for moving mass out from the centre,
>> compared with moving mass toward the centre.
>
>I don't follow the "time wind" business, could you explain?.  Sounds
>wonderful, though.  The advantages I can see for the centre are inertia,
>history and the ability to throw rocks on colonists "lower" down, as you
>described.

I gave the example of weapons taking 1 empire year out and 200 empire
years in.  Substitute weapon for cargo ship, and that's what I meant.  

