Date: Fri, 09 Apr 93 21:51:37 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Hal Finney asks for clarification on the chronology protection
mechanism.  If an enormous time shift has been created between the
widely separated mouths of a hole what is the mechanism that prevents
them from approaching eachother (and so allowing the construction of a
time machine)?   Surely if the holes are separated by thousands
of light years no energy flux could cross that distance?  Or repulsion
operate across such spans?  I'm afraid I can only partially answer Hal's
query.

As I understand what Visser has written, yes, this is what he is
proposing.  Do check the article out (perhaps you already have) and see
if it says the same to you as it does to Robin and me.  I agree it is
rather hard to swallow and we need to look carefully at and be very
suspicious of what he is saying.  Equally we must be careful not to
reject things just because they seem unintuitive.  Two holes, with a
time shift of 29,990 years, separated by 29,980 ly rather than 30,000
ly sound rather similar, I agree.  But in relativistic terms there's a
world of difference - in the former case a light ray can trace a path
through normal space-time and through the holes to make a closed
temporal loop, whereas in the latter case it can't.

Another, more abstract and generic way of viewing the process says that
where we have a space-time topology that allows temporal loops we have
singular solutions or positive feedback.  Like an audio system where the
output is feeding into the mike.  A very small change in the position
between mike and speaker can a make a large difference in the solution
(howling or silence).  As the gain becomes greater than one the noise
level is limited only by the capacity of the system.  The interesting
thing is that measurable effects occur before the system goes into
feedback.  Visser seems to be saying that it is these pre-feedback
effects that prevent the feedback solutions being reached.  In the
wormhole arrangement the local vacuum fluctuations suddenly find they
can 'borrow' energy from themselves, via Mr Heisenberg, and take on a
new non-ephemeral life to form temporal loops.  Their contribution to
the stress-energy tensor overloads the wormhole which collapses and the
singularity avoided.

Can such a flux of vacuum fluctuations cross thousands of light years? 
I don't know, but I sure as hell would like to try the experiment!

