I have to jump in here.  I disagree that you can't teach Kanji
phonetically, primarily because each Kanji does map to a limited set of
sounds.  I'll address Japanese since that's what I know about.  I don't
know directly about learning Chinese, but I believe some of what I have to
say will also apply.

First, let me briefly describe the Japanese writing system for those who
may not be familiar with its nuances.  Written Japanese was first developed
relatively early in the Common Era; about 100 C.E. but I'm not entirely
certain.  It was derived from the Chinese ideographic system, but evolved
quite differently from there.  It has two syllabaries
called hiragana and katakana or kana collectively.  (Note that `k' and `g'
are the unvoiced and voiced forms of the same fundamental phoneme.)  It
also has a set of ~2000 ideograms called the Kanji.

The kana form a syllabary rather than an alphabet because each symbol
corresponds to a complete syllable rather than to (approximately) a
phoneme.  Each set of kana (hiragana and katakana) consists of 50 symbols
which map to the sounds of the spoken language.  Each set contains
equivalent characters which map to the same syllables; the only difference
in the two sets is how they were derived (irrelevant to the discussion) and
how they are used in the language.  Hiragana is used primarily to represent
grammatical constructs such as structure words and endings.  It is also
used to write out the sounds of Kanji to help the reader.  (This usage is
called furigana.)  Katakana is used primarily to represent loan words
adopted into Japanese.  For example, almost all computer related words will
be spelled using katakana.  Katakana can also be used to provide emphasis
much as we use italics.  Note that the kana symbols are fairly simple,
consisting generally of two strokes for the simple sounds up to four
strokes for the more complex sounds.  

The Kanji are the ~2000 ideograms which are composed to form the root words
of Japanese.  Kanji are much more complex symbols, formed from one to as
many as eleven (maybe more, this is all off the top of my head) strokes.
The average is probably five or six.  The important point about Kanji is
that each character can represent multiple sounds, but the number of
possible readings is limited and in most cases is just two.  This is
because when the Japanese borrowed the ideograms, they generally mapped
each character to both a spoken Japanese word which gave the character a
meaning, which gave it one sound, and they retained one of the original
Chinese pronunciations to give it a sound independent of its meaning.  Note
that these sounds are not necessarily individual syllables but generally
consist of one or two syllables.

So what this means is that there is a phonetic basis underlying the
Japanese writing system and that in fact, there is a basis for learning
written Japanese in a manner analogous to the phonics system.

As I understand it, Japanese children are first taught hiragana, and that
early children's books are written entirely in hiragana.  Because the
mapping from hiragana to sounds is so direct (unlike the mapping from the
English alphabet to English sounds) there is no reason to learn to read
hiragana in any way other than analogously to phonics.  

Next, Japanese children start learning individual Kanji by having their
sounds explained and by having the sound written in furigana next to or on
top of the Kanji.  I have a Japanese children's book at home that
introduces simple Kanji (1, 2, 3, up, down, left, right) using this method.
So eventually Japanese children learn the common readings for the common
Kanji and can thus sound out words that they may not have encountered
before.  This sounding out is somewhat complex because each character
generally has at least two sounds, but most words consist of only two
characters, plus there are general rules about which sound is used in what
case.  

So to summarize, I believe that not only can Japanese be taught using a
method analogous to phonics, but that there is evidence that that is the
way it's taught, at least at lower levels.

This leads into the crux of what I believe underlies the phonics vs.
look-say controversy, namely the interpretation/compilation dichotomy, but
I've already spent too long here as it is, so you all will have to wait
until Monday for that.

